\textbf{H1 (problem, from original H1):} When hunting for samples in full-length recordings (e.g., on YouTube or Samplette), producers spend substantial time listening to and manually scrubbing through songs whose sections do not match their search goals (such as finding a clean instrumental passage without vocals or heavy drums). & $\geq$60\% of sampling producers describe listening or scrubbing through songs that don’t have the part they need as a recurring reason for slow digging (Q5, Q6, 6a). & Finding the right part takes them little effort, for example because auto-chop tools or stem separation make it quick (6b). \\
\hline
\textbf{H2 (solution, from original H2):} Producers rely primarily on objective categories (instruments, section type/breaks, tempo) while distrusting subjective mood/texture tags. & $\geq$60\% name objective categories as the ones they rely on, and describe mood or texture tags as unreliable when they can’t hear the sound (7a, 8a). & They rely on mood or texture tags as much as on objective ones, or use no categories at all. \\
\hline
\textbf{H3 (solution, from original H3):} Producers who save songs online to sample later, like in a YouTube or Spotify playlist, struggle to keep track of which part they wanted and why they saved it. & $\geq$60\% of those who save songs to sample later can’t remember the part or the reason without relistening (Q9, 9a). & Most remember the part, or already note the timestamp when they save. \\
\hline
\textbf{H4 (problem, from original H4):} Small-scale producers actively prefer searching for unlicensed, full-length music in streaming service catalogs (e.g., YouTube, SoundCloud) over paid pre-curated libraries (Splice, Tracklib) due to cost, catalog variety, and commercial sample-pack overuse. & $\geq$60\% regularly sample from YouTube or other public streams without being put off by clearance, and most name cost, catalog variety or pack overuse when asked why (4a). Partly confirmed if the preference holds but the reasons differ. & Most mainly use royalty-free packs and avoid YouTube recordings because of copyright worries. \\
\hline
\textbf{H5 (solution, from original H3):} Producers are willing to publicize their saved tags and timestamps on song sections (rather than keeping all discovered sources strictly private). & $\geq$60\% have shared sample sources before or keep their online saves public (Q10, 10a), and few describe keeping finds secret (10b). & Most keep their saves private and have never shared a find. This triggers the pivot to a personal crate-digging workspace. \\
\hline
\textbf{H6 (solution, from original H2):} Producers rarely get a usable sample from their first search; they find better ones by following what they’ve already heard. & $\geq$60\% rarely find a usable sample in their first search or track (Q5, 5a), and describe something they’d heard guiding where they looked next (Q8). & Most find what they need on the first search, or say what they’d heard didn’t change where they looked. \\
\hline
